From: Noa Himesaka <himesaka@noa.codes>
Subject: [PATCH] thermal: qcom: add the QMI remote temperature sensors

Qualcomm remote processors report temperature sensors of their own over
the QMI Thermal Sensor service (0x17): the modem's thermistors next to
its power amplifiers and RF front end, for instance. Add a driver that
registers each sensor named in the device tree as a thermal zone sensor,
ported from Qualcomm's downstream qmi_sensors driver: the same binding,
sensor table and messages.

As downstream, a zone is registered when the remote lists its sensor and
reports the last reading while the remote is down. Unlike downstream, a
read waits for the current-temperature report the request triggers
instead of returning the previous one, and temperatures are decoded from
the service's IEEE 754 values to the millidegree instead of truncated to
whole degrees.

Signed-off-by: Noa Himesaka <himesaka@noa.codes>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---

diff --git a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/thermal/qcom,qmi-sensors.yaml b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/thermal/qcom,qmi-sensors.yaml
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..7460f88
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/thermal/qcom,qmi-sensors.yaml
@@ -0,0 +1,64 @@
+# SPDX-License-Identifier: (GPL-2.0-only OR BSD-2-Clause)
+%YAML 1.2
+---
+$id: http://devicetree.org/schemas/thermal/qcom,qmi-sensors.yaml#
+$schema: http://devicetree.org/meta-schemas/core.yaml#
+
+title: Qualcomm QMI remote temperature sensors
+
+maintainers:
+  - Noa Himesaka <himesaka@noa.codes>
+
+description:
+  Qualcomm remote processors such as the modem own thermistors, next to the
+  power amplifiers and the RF front end for instance, and report them through
+  the QMI Thermal Sensor service. A child node per remote processor lists the
+  sensors to use by their QMI names. The thermal sensor specifier is the
+  sensor's index in the driver's table of QMI names plus the instance id.
+
+allOf:
+  - $ref: thermal-sensor.yaml#
+
+properties:
+  compatible:
+    const: qcom,qmi-sensors
+
+  "#thermal-sensor-cells":
+    const: 1
+
+patternProperties:
+  "^(modem|adsp|cdsp|slpi)$":
+    type: object
+    additionalProperties: false
+    description: One remote processor.
+
+    properties:
+      qcom,instance-id:
+        $ref: /schemas/types.yaml#/definitions/uint32
+        description: QMI service instance of the remote processor.
+
+      qcom,qmi-sensor-names:
+        $ref: /schemas/types.yaml#/definitions/string-array
+        description: QMI names of the sensors to register.
+
+    required:
+      - qcom,instance-id
+      - qcom,qmi-sensor-names
+
+required:
+  - compatible
+  - "#thermal-sensor-cells"
+
+unevaluatedProperties: false
+
+examples:
+  - |
+    qmi-ts-sensors {
+        compatible = "qcom,qmi-sensors";
+        #thermal-sensor-cells = <1>;
+
+        modem {
+            qcom,instance-id = <0>;
+            qcom,qmi-sensor-names = "pa", "pa_1", "mmw_pa1", "mmw_pa3";
+        };
+    };
diff --git a/drivers/thermal/qcom/Kconfig b/drivers/thermal/qcom/Kconfig
index a8cf7e2..a0d715f 100644
--- a/drivers/thermal/qcom/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/thermal/qcom/Kconfig
@@ -65,3 +65,13 @@ config QCOM_LMH
 	  input from temperature and current sensors.  On many newer Qualcomm SoCs
 	  LMh is configured in the firmware and this feature need not be enabled.
 	  However, on certain SoCs like sdm845 LMh has to be configured from kernel.
+
+config QCOM_QMI_TS
+	tristate "Qualcomm QMI remote temperature sensors"
+	depends on ARCH_QCOM || COMPILE_TEST
+	depends on QCOM_QMI_HELPERS && NET
+	help
+	  This enables the temperature sensors that Qualcomm remote processors,
+	  such as the modem, report over the QMI Thermal Sensor service: the
+	  thermistors next to the modem's power amplifiers and RF front end.
+	  Each sensor listed in the device tree becomes a thermal zone sensor.
